# The package installs the session and its systemd user units. It deliberately
# does not edit /etc/greetd: installing a package should not change which
# session the greeter starts. Making Cosmalia the default is an explicit step.
post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Cosmalia is installed. It was NOT set as the default session: this package
does not edit /etc/greetd.

Greeters that read /usr/share/wayland-sessions (Noctalia Greeter, GDM, SDDM)
already list it. gtkgreet only lists /etc/greetd/environments plus its -c
argument. To make Cosmalia selectable without changing the default:

  sudo cosmalia-session-default --list

To make it the default, run:
  sudo cosmalia-session-default
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  if grep -qs '/usr/local/bin/cosmic-comp-traffic-session' \
      /etc/greetd/config.toml /etc/greetd/environments 2>/dev/null; then
    cat <<'EOF'
The old cosmic-comp-traffic-session launcher is still referenced by the greeter
but its package was replaced, so that entry no longer exists. Remove the -c
argument or the /etc/greetd/environments line, or run (this also makes Cosmalia
the default):

  sudo cosmalia-session-default
EOF
  fi
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Removing this package does not change /etc/greetd. If Cosmalia is the greeter
default, point the greeter at another session first, otherwise it will try to
start a command that no longer exists.

  sudo cp -a /etc/greetd/config.toml.bak.<timestamp> /etc/greetd/config.toml

(or edit the -c argument / environments list by hand).
EOF
}
